      ["Withhold stress ulcer prophylaxis because the patient lacks guideline-directed indications, and routine acid suppression increases risks of Clostridioides difficile colitis and nosocomial pneumonia", "Stress ulcer prophylaxis (SUP) with proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) or histamine-2 receptor antagonists (H2RAs) is widely and inappropriately over-prescribed on general medical inpatient wards. According to the American Society of Health-System Pharmacists (ASHP) and Surviving Sepsis guidelines, STRESS ULCER PROPHYLAXIS IS INDICATED STRICTLY FOR HIGH-RISK CRITICALLY ILL PATIENTS IN THE INTENSIVE CARE UNIT (ICU) WHO HAVE: (1) MECHANICAL VENTILATION FOR > 48 HOURS, or (2) COAGULOPATHY (platelet count < 50,000/mm3, INR > 1.5, or PTT > 2x control). Minor ICU risk factors requiring at least two criteria include sepsis, occult GI bleeding for >= 6 days, ICU stay > 1 week, or high-dose corticosteroids. In non-ICU patients on general medical floors who are eating and have no coagulopathy or mechanical ventilation, THE RISK OF CLINICALLY SIGNIFICANT BLEEDING IS < 0.1% TO 0.2%. Prescribing routine PPI prophylaxis in general medical patients provides zero clinical benefit while significantly INCREASING THE RISKS OF CLOSTRIDIOIDES DIFFICILE COLITIS, HOSPITAL-ACQUIRED PNEUMONIA, acute interstitial nephritis, and drug interactions."],
