      ["Administer inhaled nebulized racemic epinephrine and intravenous dexamethasone, while keeping immediate re-intubation equipment at the bedside", "The patient has developed acute post-extubation stridor secondary to laryngeal edema (glottic and subglottic mucosal edema caused by mechanical pressure and irritation from the translaryngeal endotracheal tube). Risk factors include prolonged mechanical ventilation (> 36–48 hr), female sex, and a low pre-extubation cuff leak (< 110 mL or < 10–24% of tidal volume). Acute post-extubation stridor signals critical upper airway narrowing that can rapidly progress to complete asphyxiation. THE EVIDENCE-BASED IMMEDIATE MEDICAL MANAGEMENT INCLUDES: (1) NEBULIZED RACEMIC EPINEPHRINE (or L-epinephrine): Epinephrine stimulates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on mucosal arterioles, causing rapid local vasoconstriction, reducing capillary permeability, and shrinking glottic mucosal edema within 10 to 30 minutes; (2) INTRAVENOUS CORTICOSTEROIDS (dexamethasone or methylprednisolone) to reduce delayed and ongoing inflammatory swelling; and (3) AIRWAY READINESS: Prepare for immediate emergent re-intubation (with a smaller endotracheal tube and video laryngoscopy) if the patient develops severe respiratory distress, somnolence, exhaustion, or worsening hypoxemia refractory to nebulized epinephrine."],
      ["Administer continuous nebulized albuterol every 15 minutes to dilate the extrathoracic trachea", "Albuterol is a selective beta-2 agonist that relaxes lower airway bronchial smooth muscle; it has zero alpha-1 vasoconstrictive activity and is completely ineffective for upper airway glottic mucosal edema."],
